<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Simuka</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3rd–1st century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Simuka is named in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puranas">Puranas</a> as the founder of the Satavahana dynasty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan">Deccan</a>, and his name appears in a label beside a now-damaged relief portrait in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naneghat">Naneghat</a> cave. The Puranic lists credit him with overthrowing the last Kanva ruler, but his date is uncertain, with scholars placing his reign anywhere from the late 3rd to the 1st century BCE. He was succeeded by his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/simuka.jpg" alt="Simuka" width="320" height="269" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photograph by Kevin Standage Rubbing by J. Burgess (1883, d. 1892) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Simuka_inscription_at_Naneghat_Caves.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
